          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Your triage tool rated this as routine, and no emergency pattern matched. That does not mean nothing is serious, so the warning signs below still matter. Seek urgent care if you have fever with back or side pain, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, or confusion. If fever, a fast heartbeat, low blood pressure or confusion occur together, that can signal sepsis, which is an emergency: call emergency services now (red-flag-sepsis). Also seek urgent care if you are not passing urine for many hours, feel dizzy on standing or very drowsy, or if your symptoms get worse or do not improve within about 48 hours of treatment (condition-urinary-tract-infection, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment). Any infection while pregnant, or if you have one kidney or a transplanted kidney, needs prompt assessment.\n\nThree infections in a year is reasonable to raise with a clinician. The guidance defines recurrence as \"two or more in six months or three or more in a year\" (guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment), so your pattern meets that conventional definition. That is not a diagnosis, and a clinician would confirm it. The same guidance says urine culture is indicated for recurrent infection, so a clinician may want a sample tested. Factors a clinician may look at include incomplete bladder emptying, kidney stones, diabetes, catheters and previous infections (condition-urinary-tract-infection). The guidance also treats infections in men as \"complicated by default,\" which may matter depending on your sex. The sources do not give a full workup pathway, so I cannot say what a clinician would order.\n\nFor prevention, the guidance describes behaviour changes, vaginal oestrogen after menopause, and, where recurrence is frequent and disabling, antibiotic prophylaxis, either continuous or after intercourse. Prophylaxis has to be weighed against the risk of antibiotic resistance. Cranberry products and higher fluid intake are described as having \"limited and mixed evidence,\" so they are the weakest options. The sources do not grade the evidence for each option, and they do not cover methenamine or vaccines, so ask a clinician or check a dedicated prevention guideline for those.\n\nThe sources also disagree on early treatment. Some European guidance supports offering symptom relief with delayed or no antibiotics for mild cystitis, as a shared decision. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, citing symptom duration and the risk of kidney involvement. The disagreement is about how much antibiotic exposure is worth avoiding, not about the underlying facts. The sources do not say where that line falls for prophylaxis.\n\nBook an appointment and bring a record of each of the three episodes: dates, symptoms and any treatment you had. Ask whether a urine culture is needed, whether a check for a structural or metabolic factor would help, and which prevention options suit you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
